L166: Within a test, **throw**, not `exit`; the script fails on CLI errors, empty inspections, and undelivered evidence. Screenshots target the known main HWND so each lands at one exact host path; use `--capture-screen` when popups must be included.
L167: ### Step 3: Run, read, and visually verify
L168: 
L169: ```powershell
L170: # Preferred when Windows Sandbox is available:
L171: .\ui-tests.ps1 -Target sandbox
L172: if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw 'UI batch failed; read test-results.json.' }
L173: $report = Get-Content -LiteralPath '.\test-results.json' -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json
L174: $report.screenshots
L175: ```
L176: For a captured, still-live guest PID, use `.\ui-tests.ps1 -Target sandbox -AppPid 1234 -AppScope sandbox`. For a local run (see Step 1), use `.\ui-tests.ps1 -Target local`, optionally with a local PID and `-AppScope local`. Never reuse a guest PID locally or switch targets to make failing tests pass.
L177: View **each host PNG** listed in `test-results.json` with the image-viewing tool. UIA PASS cannot detect clipping, overlap, incorrect theming, or content bleeding past its container. Fail visual review for unintended scrollbars, unintended ellipses, clipped hero/right-edge controls, overlapping rows, unbalanced whitespace, cramped/vast spacing, incorrect Light/Dark/High Contrast, or missing focus/hover/error states. Capture meaningful states immediately after their interactions, not just at the end.
L178: If fixes are requested, batch-fix failures, rebuild/relaunch with the same target using the template (omit `-AppPid` to launch again), and capture the new PID. Maximum **two** fix-and-rerun cycles; then report remaining failures. Do not declare completion without the structured results **and** visual review.
L179: ### Assertions and coverage
L180: 
L181: Write tests for every requested requirement. Use `wait-for --value` for TextBox/NumberBox values, RichEditBox text, ComboBox selection, toggle/checkbox `On`/`Off`, and TextBlock/label text. `--contains` matches a substring; `--gone` waits for disappearance. Use `-p IsEnabled --value True` only for a specific property. Invoke buttons/nav items, wait for page-specific elements, and test default/error/empty states.
L182: The following examples are additions to the template, not independent shells. `Invoke-Ui` always supplies the chosen scope plus `-a $AppPid`, or scope plus `-w $Window`. For raw reads, pass `--json` and parse it; plain stdout can include advisory messages. Use `scroll-into-view` before asserting a virtualized item below the fold.
L183: ```powershell
L184: Test-UI 'Status and enabled state' {
L185:     Invoke-Ui @('wait-for', 'StatusBar', '--value', 'saved', '--contains', '-t', '3000')
L186:     Invoke-Ui @('wait-for', 'BtnSave', '-p', 'IsEnabled', '--value', 'True', '-t', '3000')
L187:     $value = Invoke-Ui @('get-value', 'TxtUserName', '--json') | ConvertFrom-Json
L188:     if ($value.text -ne 'TestUser') { throw 'Wrong username.' }
L189: }
L190: ```
L191: ### File pickers, flyouts, and ContentDialogs
L192: 
L193: Pickers run in a separate `PickerHost` process. Discover their HWND in the **same target**; `-w` changes the window selector, never the target. Replace the filename with a file that exists **in the guest** for Sandbox testing.
L194: ```powershell
L195: Test-UI 'Open file picker' {
L196:     Invoke-Ui @('invoke', 'BtnOpenFile')
L197:     Start-Sleep -Seconds 1
L198:     $allWindows = @(Invoke-WinAppChecked (@('ui', 'list-windows', '--json') + $ScopeArgs) | ConvertFrom-Json)
L199:     $pickers = @($allWindows | Where-Object { $_.hwnd -and [string]$_.ownerHwnd -eq $hwnd -and $_.title -match 'Open|Save' })
L200:     if ($pickers.Count -ne 1) { throw 'Expected one picker; inspect the selected target again.' }
L201:     $pickerHwnd = [string]$pickers[0].hwnd
L202:     Invoke-Ui @('inspect', '--interactive', '--json') -Window $pickerHwnd
L203:     Invoke-Ui @('set-value', 'FileNameControlHost', 'test.txt') -Window $pickerHwnd
L204:     Invoke-Ui @('invoke', 'Open') -Window $pickerHwnd
L205:     Invoke-Ui @('wait-for', 'StatusBar', '--value', 'opened', '--contains', '-t', '3000')
L206: }
L207: Test-UI 'Copy context menu' {
L208:     Invoke-Ui @('click', 'LstItems', '--right')
L209:     Invoke-Ui @('wait-for', 'MnuCopy', '-t', '2000')
L210:     Invoke-Ui @('invoke', 'MnuCopy')
L211:     Invoke-Ui @('wait-for', 'StatusText', '--value', 'Copied', '-t', '2000')
L212: }
L213: 
L214: Test-UI 'Confirm deletion' {
L215:     Invoke-Ui @('invoke', 'BtnDelete')
L216:     Invoke-Ui @('wait-for', 'Primary', '-t', '2000')
L217:     Invoke-Ui @('invoke', 'Primary')
L218:     Invoke-Ui @('wait-for', 'Primary', '--gone', '-t', '3000')
L219: }
L220: ```
L221: For Save/Cancel pickers, substitute the appropriate action and assertion. MenuBar headers and flyout items use `invoke` and `wait-for` similarly. ContentDialogs live in the app window; `Primary`, `Secondary`, and `Close` are common selectors, but inspect the actual open dialog because custom AutomationIds are not guaranteed.
L222: ### Keyboard, hover, drag, touch, and pen
L223: 
L224: `send-keys --via send-input` is required for accelerators and reliable per-character input in WinUI/WPF text controls. `post-message` is HWND-targeted but does not produce per-character `KeyDown`. `--target` focuses first; `--verbatim` types literal key names. System shortcuts require the CLI's explicit permission flags; do not use them as a workaround for desktop-readiness errors.
L225: ```powershell
L226: Test-UI 'Keyboard save' {
L227:     Invoke-Ui @('send-keys', 'hello world', '--target', 'TxtName', '--via', 'send-input')
L228:     Invoke-Ui @('send-keys', 'ctrl+s', '--via', 'send-input')
L229:     Invoke-Ui @('wait-for', 'StatusBar', '--value', 'saved', '--contains', '-t', '2000')
L230: }
L231: Test-UI 'Tooltip' {
L232:     Invoke-Ui @('hover', 'BtnInfo')
L233:     Invoke-Ui @('wait-for', 'InfoTooltip', '-t', '2000')
L234: }
L235: Test-UI 'Reorder items' {
L236:     Invoke-Ui @('drag', 'ItemA', 'ItemB')
L237:     Invoke-Ui @('wait-for', 'StatusBar', '--value', 'reordered', '--contains', '-t', '2000')
L238: }
